next thing you know like weird things are happening to your stock sales,

00:13:51.990 --> 00:13:54.080
they're getting reversed and, and whatever.

00:13:54.080 --> 00:13:56.910
Uh, and well, you know, nobody's come out and threatened you.

00:13:56.910 --> 00:13:59.460
But you know that there are a lot of miners in China and like you know,

00:13:59.460 --> 00:14:02.560
this is just could be a very uncomfortable situation to be in.

00:14:02.560 --> 00:14:05.490
Okay, so the moral of the story here is we have

00:14:05.490 --> 00:14:09.695
to protect asset issuers from forks in the Blockchain.

00:14:09.695 --> 00:14:13.085
And so the obvious idea here is why don't you require whoever's

00:14:13.085 --> 00:14:16.330
issuing an asset to endorse all the transactions in that asset, right?

00:14:16.330 --> 00:14:17.930
After all, you know, the issuer is

00:14:17.930 --> 00:14:20.425
a counterparty to these digital tokens, so they should,

00:14:20.425 --> 00:14:22.240
you know be allowed to know, you know,

00:14:22.240 --> 00:14:24.660
who's transferring the the money and make sure that

00:14:24.660 --> 00:14:27.250
it's not- that the the coins aren't getting duplicated.

00:14:27.250 --> 00:14:30.425
So you could kind of call this the PayPal model, right.

00:14:30.425 --> 00:14:32.770
You've got this centrally managed ledger like

00:14:32.770 --> 00:14:36.080
PayPal keeping track of who has all these tokens and obviously,

00:14:36.080 --> 00:14:38.180
PayPal doesn't face the threat of

00:14:38.180 --> 00:14:43.130
double claims on redemption because because everything happens through them.

00:14:43.130 --> 00:14:46.780
But if we went to a world with kind of a thousand PayPal's, you know,

00:14:46.780 --> 00:14:48.620
you have a PayPal for your transit rides,

00:14:48.620 --> 00:14:50.040
a PayPal for your cell phone minutes,

00:14:50.040 --> 00:14:51.810
a PayPal, you know,

00:14:51.810 --> 00:14:53.540
for for each of these things.

00:14:53.540 --> 00:14:57.900
The problem is that you then can't trade across these different PayPal's, right?

00:14:57.900 --> 00:15:00.600
So this rules out even basic forex trading.

00:15:00.600 --> 00:15:03.080
And I would argue that most of the kind of,

00:15:03.080 --> 00:15:05.295
the most compelling applications of watching

00:15:05.295 --> 00:15:08.475
involve introducing new token types and being able to transact,

00:15:08.475 --> 00:15:11.895
have atomic transactions that involve multiple different token types.

00:15:11.895 --> 00:15:14.995
So, the idea that we we had, uh,

00:15:14.995 --> 00:15:18.580
with Stellar which is a nonprofit I'm involved with is,

00:15:18.580 --> 00:15:21.300
what if we have many issuers issuing tokens,

00:15:21.300 --> 00:15:23.085
but all on one ledger.

00:15:23.085 --> 00:15:30.060
However, each issuer is going to define tokens in such a way as to prevent double claims.

00:15:30.060 --> 00:15:32.835
And so what we're going to do is we're going to have the issuers,

00:15:32.835 --> 00:15:34.660
each issuer is going to de- designate

00:15:34.660 --> 00:15:39.710
a validator authority who is kind of authoritative for one portion of the ledger,

00:15:39.710 --> 00:15:43.630
namely the holdings in that issuer's, uh, tokens, right.

00:15:43.630 --> 00:15:45.580
And then all the different validators are going to

00:15:45.580 --> 00:15:48.125
cooperate to maintain the single-global ledger,

00:15:48.125 --> 00:15:49.555
but if history did fork,

00:15:49.555 --> 00:15:51.400
there'd be no ambiguity as to, you know,

00:15:51.400 --> 00:15:54.080
which version of the ledger was authoritative for,

00:15:54.080 --> 00:15:55.330
uh, for which token type,

00:15:55.330 --> 00:15:57.085
because you kind of declared that in advance.

00:15:57.085 --> 00:15:58.850
And these validators, they're known parties,

00:15:58.850 --> 00:16:00.020
they're not anonymous at all.

00:16:00.020 --> 00:16:02.170
They're things like banks or you know,

00:16:02.170 --> 00:16:04.865
big companies like IBM for example.

00:16:04.865 --> 00:16:07.745
So, the idea here is that, well,

00:16:07.745 --> 00:16:13.240
we already trust the token issuers uh, to remain solvent and to be willing to redeem tokens,

00:16:13.240 --> 00:16:15.040
because that's what gives the tokens value.

00:16:15.040 --> 00:16:20.530
So, why don't we also trust the token issuers to make the transactions irreversible.

00:16:20.530 --> 00:16:23.010
And uh,  in order to do this,

00:16:23.010 --> 00:16:24.460
we need to make, uh, well,

00:16:24.460 --> 00:16:27.070
so so the idea here would be that,

00:16:27.070 --> 00:16:28.450
imagine that, you know,

00:16:28.450 --> 00:16:30.595
you're only gonna agree that a transaction happens when

00:16:30.595 --> 00:16:32.930
all the sort of counterparties you care about do.

00:16:32.930 --> 00:16:36.090
So, if you're interested in cashing out dollars at bank one

00:16:36.090 --> 00:16:39.575
and rupees at bank four then you'll say like, hey,

00:16:39.575 --> 00:16:41.660
I'm only going to agree to a transaction when, you know,

00:16:41.660 --> 00:16:44.520
my bank in San Francisco and this other bank here in Mumbai,

00:16:44.520 --> 00:16:46.790
India have both agreed that this has happened.

00:16:46.790 --> 00:16:49.100
But these banks deal with other banks, right.

00:16:49.100 --> 00:16:53.270
And so, they're not going to agree that a transaction's happened until

00:16:53.270 --> 00:16:57.610
the banks that they depend on agreed it's happened who also will depend on other banks,

00:16:57.610 --> 00:17:02.550
and so on, until you kind of take the transitive closure of these dependency relations.

00:17:02.550 --> 00:17:05.420
And eventually, the whole world has agreed that this transaction has happened.

00:17:05.420 --> 00:17:07.790
And so, at this point, once everybody's agreed,

00:17:07.790 --> 00:17:10.540
it becomes very hard to roll this back, and even if,

00:17:10.540 --> 00:17:13.450
if you remember an example I had bank 2, we didn't know who it was,

00:17:13.450 --> 00:17:16.390
but it was offering us a good rate getting from Dollars to Euros.

00:17:16.390 --> 00:17:19.680
And so, if bank 2 somehow decides the transaction didn't happen,

00:17:19.680 --> 00:17:22.350
but the whole rest of the world does, well who cares, you know,

00:17:22.350 --> 00:17:27.100
because at the end of the day you don't care about cashing anything in at bank two,

00:17:27.100 --> 00:17:30.115
and so bank two could just be in its own weird little fork of the world.

00:17:30.115 --> 00:17:31.905
Or if wants to exist,

00:17:31.905 --> 00:17:35.810
and continue to exist in the banking system it'll have to agree that that this has happened.

00:17:35.810 --> 00:17:39.960
So, uh, so in order to make this happen,

00:17:39.960 --> 00:17:44.115
we need one assumption and this is something that I call, The Internet hypothesis.

00:17:44.115 --> 00:17:48.680
Right. So, the Internet is this globally connected network,

00:17:48.680 --> 00:17:50.840
but what's interesting is that there's no central authority

00:17:50.840 --> 00:17:53.245
that's dictating the structure of the Internet, right.

00:17:53.245 --> 00:17:55.340
Instead basically, the Internet has arisen out of

00:17:55.340 --> 00:17:58.120
these pairwise relationships between networks that,

00:17:58.120 --> 00:18:01.695
uh, enter into peering and, and transit agreements, right.

00:18:01.695 --> 00:18:05.885
And so, of course, this decentralized and peering structure

00:18:05.885 --> 00:18:08.390
could have resulted in multiple different Internets,

00:18:08.390 --> 00:18:09.460
but it didn't, right.

00:18:09.460 --> 00:18:12.045
It resulted in this one network that we call the Internet.

00:18:12.045 --> 00:18:15.915
And t he reason is that sort of transitively, everybody wants to talk to everyone, right?

00:18:15.915 --> 00:18:17.540
I may not know who you are, but you know,

00:18:17.540 --> 00:18:19.530
I want to talk to say, Google.

00:18:19.530 --> 00:18:20.845
Or you want to talk to Google or I want

00:18:20.845 --> 00:18:22.430
to talk to somebody who wants to talk to Google, and so on.

00:18:22.430 --> 00:18:25.900
And if you take sort of the, the transitive convergence of everybody,

00:18:25.900 --> 00:18:27.630
people want to talk to, uh,

00:18:27.630 --> 00:18:29.715
you'll get kind of a lot of overlap.

00:18:29.715 --> 00:18:34.840
So, the hypothesis here is that counterparty relationships in,

00:18:34.840 --> 00:18:37.755
uh, in these tokens are actually,

00:18:37.755 --> 00:18:40.320
uh, you know, you have the same kind of a transitive convergence, right.

00:18:40.320 --> 00:18:42.930
So, you have banks dealing with correspondent banks,

00:18:42.930 --> 00:18:44.380
dealing with clearing houses and essentially,

00:18:44.380 --> 00:18:48.090
these clearing houses are going to be like the the tier one ISPs of payments.

00:18:48.090 --> 00:18:50.670
So, using this idea,

00:18:50.670 --> 00:18:52.930
we can define a, uh,

00:18:52.930 --> 00:18:55.290
a new kind of consensus, uh,

00:18:55.290 --> 00:18:58.520
protocol that I call Federated Byzantine Agreement.

00:18:58.520 --> 00:19:01.430
And the idea is based on Byzantine Agreement,

00:19:01.430 --> 00:19:06.285
which is a known technique for achieving consensus amongst a closed group of nodes.

00:19:06.285 --> 00:19:09.100
But the problem with classic Byzantine Agreement is that it

00:19:09.100 --> 00:19:11.880
bases security on this notion of of,

00:19:11.880 --> 00:19:14.020
uh, of majorities, right,

00:19:14.020 --> 00:19:15.840
or or supermajorities, right.

00:19:15.840 --> 00:19:21.025
So, uh, so, it's vulnerable to this attack called the Sybil attack where you say, hey,

00:19:21.025 --> 00:19:22.910
any like two-thirds plus one of nodes

00:19:22.910 --> 00:19:25.320
constitute a quorum and so a bad guy comes in and creates,

00:19:25.320 --> 00:19:28.080
you know, a hundred fake nodes that it all controls,

00:19:28.080 --> 00:19:30.030
and so it overwhelms the number of good nodes.

00:19:30.030 --> 00:19:33.360
So, the idea is we're going to defeat these Sybil attacks, uh,

00:19:33.360 --> 00:19:35.590
by choosing quorum composition in

00:19:35.590 --> 00:19:38.900
a decentralized way and this will be much less symmetric,

00:19:38.900 --> 00:19:43.360
but it will depend on the actual trust that people place in the entities in the system.

00:19:43.360 --> 00:19:47.610
So, if this works, each node V is going to pick one or more sets of nodes that,

00:19:47.610 --> 00:19:49.710
uh, we call quorum slices,

00:19:49.710 --> 00:19:53.500
and V basically picks as a quorum slice any set of nodes

00:19:53.500 --> 00:19:57.310
that it considers important enough to kind of speak for the network as a whole, right.

00:19:57.310 --> 00:20:00.200
So, the choice is going to be based on real-world identities, but in particular,

00:20:00.200 --> 00:20:05.040
if you care about redeeming tokens issued by by particular nodes,

00:20:05.040 --> 00:20:09.025
you probably want to put those nodes in all of your quorum slices.

00:20:09.025 --> 00:20:10.700
So, given this idea,

00:20:10.700 --> 00:20:13.040
we can define a Federated Byzantine Agreement System

00:20:13.040 --> 00:20:15.520
as a set of nodes V and this quorum function Q

00:20:15.520 --> 00:20:20.490
where Q of little v is a set of slices chosen by node V. And now,

00:20:20.490 --> 00:20:24.105
we can define a quorum to be a set of nodes, uh,

00:20:24.105 --> 00:20:25.810
U that is a,

00:20:25.810 --> 00:20:29.210
that contains at least one quorum slice of each of its members.

00:20:29.210 --> 00:20:31.980
So, here's kind of a simple example,

00:20:31.980 --> 00:20:35.080
uh, I'll have four nodes, each one has a single quorum slice,

00:20:35.080 --> 00:20:37.750
and I'll visualize the quorum slices by drawing an arrow from

00:20:37.750 --> 00:20:40.680
a node to the other members of its quorum slice, right.

00:20:40.680 --> 00:20:44.150
And you can see that the V1 has quorum slice V1,

00:20:44.150 --> 00:20:47.460
V2, V3, the other nodes have quorum slice V2, V3, V4.

00:20:47.460 --> 00:20:49.600
So, if we consider V2, V3, V4,

00:20:49.600 --> 00:20:52.605
this is trivially a quorum because it says,

00:20:52.605 --> 00:20:56.450
it contains, it is every one of its members' quorum slices.

00:20:56.450 --> 00:20:58.140
What about V1, V2, V3?

00:20:58.140 --> 00:21:00.600
That's a quorum slice it's not a quorum because V1

00:21:00.600 --> 00:21:02.930
is saying I'll agree to anything that V2 and V3 do,

00:21:02.930 --> 00:21:05.780
but V2 and V3 say we'll only agree to something if V4 does.

00:21:05.780 --> 00:21:11.250
So, the smallest quorum including V1 is actually the set of all nodes in this example.

00:21:11.250 --> 00:21:13.885
So, here's a little more, more realistic example,

00:21:13.885 --> 00:21:16.860
have a kind of tiered quorum slice structure

00:21:16.860 --> 00:21:20.525
reminiscent of the Internet where maybe there's a top tier of quorum of, uh,

00:21:20.525 --> 00:21:22.890
of validators that depend on three out of four of each

00:21:22.890 --> 00:21:26.100
other and then maybe a middle tier that depend on two out of four the top tier,

00:21:26.100 --> 00:21:27.430
maybe there's a leaf tier.

00:21:27.430 --> 00:21:28.830
But like the Internet, there's going to be

00:21:28.830 --> 00:21:32.670
no central authority appointing the tier one validators,

00:21:32.670 --> 00:21:34.980
instead it'll be left to market choices.

00:21:34.980 --> 00:21:36.300
And maybe a market decides, hey,

00:21:36.300 --> 00:21:37.945
you know, the top four retail banks, you know,

00:21:37.945 --> 00:21:41.815
these are, these are what should constitute the th- the top tier validators.

00:21:41.815 --> 00:21:44.560
But you don't need complete consensus on this,

00:21:44.560 --> 00:21:47.150
so V7 and V8 might be paranoid, and they might say,

00:21:47.150 --> 00:21:49.490
well we realize we have to deal with the big banks,

00:21:49.490 --> 00:21:51.000
but we don't trust them.

00:21:51.000 --> 00:21:55.950
We also want to depend on some nonprofits whose in centives are differently aligned.

00:21:55.950 --> 00:21:59.170
So they'll say, we'll wait for two out of four big banks to sign off on our transactions,

00:21:59.170 --> 00:22:01.225
but also one out of these three nonprofits,

00:22:01.225 --> 00:22:04.590
and the nonprofits depend on tw- two out of three of each other.

00:22:04.590 --> 00:22:10.795
Okay, so let's say that this paranoia is well-founded and Citibank walks up to V7,

00:22:10.795 --> 00:22:13.110
pays V7 a billion dollars and says,

00:22:13.110 --> 00:22:15.140
hey, V7, I just gave you a billion dollars.

00:22:15.140 --> 00:22:16.760
We've got this Blockchain magic,

00:22:16.760 --> 00:22:18.045
so you know it's irreversible.

00:22:18.045 --> 00:22:20.650
Give me, uh, a billion dollars worth of goods.

00:22:20.650 --> 00:22:22.270
I'm going to walk away with them and you should

00:22:22.270 --> 00:22:24.315
feel secure that you got your payment, right.

00:22:24.315 --> 00:22:27.730
And so V7 says, okay, I need two out of these four big banks. Got that.

00:22:27.730 --> 00:22:30.090
I need one of these nonprofits. Got that.

00:22:30.090 --> 00:22:31.750
This nonprofit, needs another nonprofit.

00:22:31.750 --> 00:22:34.310
Got that. These guys need three out of four. Got that.

00:22:34.310 --> 00:22:36.110
Good. Everything is good.

00:22:36.110 --> 00:22:38.100
Now, it turns out, a,

00:22:38.100 --> 00:22:39.655
the paranoia was correct,

00:22:39.655 --> 00:22:41.205
and these big banks are evil,

00:22:41.205 --> 00:22:44.730
so they collude to kind of reverse the effects of this transaction.

00:22:44.730 --> 00:22:46.890
And then they walk up to V8 and say,

00:22:46.890 --> 00:22:49.000
hey, V8 here's a billion dollars that, you know,

00:22:49.000 --> 00:22:50.610
we shouldn't have because we gave it to V8,

00:22:50.610 --> 00:22:51.820
but it sure looks like we still have it,

00:22:51.820 --> 00:22:53.540
so why don't you give us a billion dollars for the goods will

00:22:53.540 --> 00:22:55.675
quickly run away with that, right?

00:22:55.675 --> 00:22:58.265
And V8 says, well, I need two out of four big banks.

00:22:58.265 --> 00:23:01.385
Check. I also need one of these nonprofits.

00:23:01.385 --> 00:23:04.030
Well, Stellar and EFF aren't going to sign off on this, right.

00:23:04.030 --> 00:23:08.150
Because they know that the money now belongs to V7,

00:23:08.150 --> 00:23:10.490
but maybe ACLU is kind of lagging behind,

00:23:10.490 --> 00:23:12.755
so V8 talks to the ACLU,

00:23:12.755 --> 00:23:16.190
but the ACLU depends on two out of these three nonprofits, so the ACLU say,

00:23:16.190 --> 00:23:19.880
well, sorry I'm not going to believe this unless one of these other nonprofits signs off.

00:23:19.880 --> 00:23:23.450
And that's not going to happen because the other nonprofits know this isn't legitimate.

00:23:23.450 --> 00:23:29.965
So those nodes will basically protect V8 against this double-spend attack.

00:23:29.965 --> 00:23:37.435
Okay. So basically I- I designed this consensus protocol based on this idea.

00:23:37.435 --> 00:23:41.530
Uh, and it has some nice properties, uh, like, uh,

00:23:41.530 --> 00:23:43.880
basically guaranteed safety when you have

00:23:43.880 --> 00:23:46.700
quorum intersection after removing all the nodes that,

00:23:46.700 --> 00:23:48.330
that deviate from the protocol.

00:23:48.330 --> 00:23:51.380
And that's a necessary property for safety in this kind of model.

00:23:51.380 --> 00:23:53.925
So what I've shown is also sufficient meaning that,

00:23:53.925 --> 00:23:56.970
you know, basically SCP kind of provides optimal safety.

00:23:56.970 --> 00:23:59.740
So, you might regret your choice of quorum slices but

00:23:59.740 --> 00:24:02.570
you won't regret choosing SCP over some other,

